1. basecaller
navigate to <storage> in terminal first
dorado basecaller hac pod5/ > basecalling_hac.bam


2. demux
navigate to <storage> in terminal first
dorado demux 	--output-dir output_demux \
		--kit-name SQK-NBD114-24 \
		--emit-fastq basecalling_hac.bam

DATA PREP for pipeline input
ls /<batch_address>/output_demux > /<R project>/raw_data/demux_batch1.csv
Open R and generate data preparation script per-batch.
Samplesheet for GHRU pipeline is stored in <R project>/inputs/ghru_samplesheet_batch<batch>.csv
Samplesheet for targeted compressed file (*.fastq.gz) is stored in <R project>/inputs/ghru_compress_batch<batch>.tsv


GHRU-mod need *.fastq.gz input instead of *.fastq (not sure why) -_-)
navigate to /<batch_address>/output_demux then compress them all first:
# or do a targeted file list like this based on the wetlab input
for f in $(cat <R project>/inputs/ghru_compress_batch<batch>.tsv); do
	gzip -kf "$f"
done

(or use while loop)
while read -r f; do
    gzip -k "$f"
done < <R project>/inputs/ghru_compress_batch<batch>.tsv


3. GHRU-mod_lambda_filter_on (use DCS2595.fasta)
DCS (DNA control sample, DCS) = SPIKE-in control. included. in. ONT. seq. kits.
I repeatedly asked what is it, people seem don't undertand what SPIKE is,
OR perhaps just a small misscommunication.
That's why the original code in Jupyter Notebook include NC_001416.1 lambda DNA.

navigate to where ghru-mod is stored
cd /srv/nfs_share/oucru_tools/GHRU-mod
conda activate nextflow
nextflow run main.nf \
	--samplesheet /<R project>/inputs/ghru_samplesheet_batch<batch>.csv \
	--outdir /<batch_address>/output_ghru-mod_lambda_filter_on \
	--remove_contamination true \
	--dcs_file /<R project>/inputs/DCS2595.fasta \
	--max_memory 100.GB \
	--max_cpus 30 \
	--medaka_model "r1041_e82_400bps_hac_v4.2.0"

note that --medaka_model "r1041_e82_400bps_hac_v4.2.0" is required.

nextflow log MUST be copied to the folder's output after running GHRU-mod.
cp /<tools directory>/GHRU-mod/.nextflow.log /<batch directory>/output_ghru-mod_lambda_filter_on/.nextflow.log

